    ],
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "post-qft-example-formal-observable-map",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Formal diagram coefficient-to-observable adoption",
        "statement": "For a specifically named finite tree calculation, adopt its selected formal\ndiagram coefficient as a **leading perturbative coefficient** with a stated\nphenomenological reading. For QED, use the vertex and free-line conventions\nof the current draft supplier\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]]. A particle of natural charge $q_n$ has\nvertex $iq_n\\gamma^a$; the electron has $q_n=-e_n$ and vertex\n$-ie_n\\gamma^a$. The rational fermion line is\n$$ i\\frac{\\not p+\\mu}{h(p,p)-\\mu^2}, $$\nfor $\\mu>0$ and off-shell $p$ with $h(p,p)-\\mu^2\\ne0$. In Feynman gauge\nthe auxiliary photon line is\n$$ -i\\frac{h_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}=i\\frac{\\eta_{ab}}{h(k,k)+i0}. $$\nFor a finite matrix kernel the coefficientwise inverse in\n[[post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients]] is part of the formal convention and\nasserts no convergence. If a time-ordered distribution is used, use the\nactual free carrier specified there; that identification is conditional on\n[[def-countable-choice]]. The displayed pole notation does not define loop\nproducts. In the pair-production channel below the selected muon also has\nmodel charge $-e$, and only the photon line is internal.\n\nFor declared incoming spin probabilities, sum over declared final spin\nlabels, and a specified formal amplitude $\\mathcal A$, adopt the\ncross-section coefficient map of\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. In the center-of-momentum frame it\nuses the invariant incoming flux $F$, the two-body measure $d\\Phi_2$, and the\nfinal-state factor $S_f=2$ for identical particles or $S_f=1$ for\ndistinguishable particles, integrated over the full labeled sphere. When\nthis coefficient is read phenomenologically, the initial preparation,\nchannel, threshold, and approximation are part of the model statement. No\nuniversal error estimate for omitted terms is implied.\n\nFor the scalar examples, separately adopt the constant formal amplitudes\n$\\mathcal A=-\\lambda$ for the quartic contact channel and $\\mathcal G=G_E$\nfor the cubic parent decay, with the units and external normalization stated\nat each use. Their coefficient map is the one in\n[[lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space]]. If a rate $\\Gamma$ is reported as\nan energy width, the convention is explicitly $\\Gamma_E:=\\hbar\\Gamma$; it\ndoes not supply a survival law.\n\nThe readings above do not identify a formal coefficient with an exact\ncontinuum cross section, probability, decay rate, or measured estimate. In\nparticular they do not construct an interacting continuum $S$-matrix or LSZ\nlimit, establish convergence of a perturbation series, bound loop or\nsoft-radiation effects, specify beam or detector cuts, resolve threshold\nresummation, or reproduce the statistical analysis of an experiment. A\ncomparison with observations would require the actual apparatus, sampling,\ncalibration, uncertainty, and radiative-inclusiveness conditions. The\nreported electron magnetic-moment measurements, including the Fan et al.\nreport listed in the source references, remain source-reported\nempirical results and are not premises of this adoption.",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-example-observable-types",
          "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients",
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-example-observable-types": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "lem-qft-example-two-body-phase-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 3 §§3.5.3 and 3.6.2–3.6.3, and Chapter 6 §6.6",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 6 §§6.4.1, 6.5 and 6.6",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S6.html"
            },
            {
              "title": "Mark Srednicki, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted prepublication draft, §§11, 46–48, 59 and 64",
              "url": "https://web.physics.ucsb.edu/~mark/ms-qft-DRAFT.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "X. Fan et al., Measurement of the Electron Magnetic Moment, Physical Review Letters 130, 071801 (2023), arXiv:2209.13084v2",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2209.13084"
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "A model-specific phenomenological reading of named formal tree coefficients through the stated two-body flux and phase-space maps. It adopts no exact continuum scattering operator, LSZ limit, convergence estimate, loop or soft-radiation correction, detector model, or empirical result.",
        "empirical_premises": [],
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "proof_strategy": "not-applicable"
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-qft-example-formal-scattering-decay",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Formal tree scattering and scalar-decay coefficients",
        "statement": "Conditionally derives the full-mass unpolarized formal e+e−→μ+μ− tree coefficient, its incoming-electron-mass comparison bound, on-shell current contractions, and the adopted scalar identical-particle scattering and decay-rate coefficients. No exact continuum scattering operator, loop/infrared error, or empirical estimate is asserted.",
